outcome success
file Makefile kept 20
| # Whether a recipe line is exec'd directly or handed to a shell is
| # `construct_command_argv_internal`'s question (job.c), and it is about the
| # whole assembled `$(SHELL) $(.SHELLFLAGS) LINE`, not the SHELL alone. GNU Make
| # builds that text and re-tokenizes it; where the tokenizer meets shell syntax
| # it takes the `goto slow` and hands the line to a shell that splits it. A
| # `.SHELLFLAGS` holding a backquote is exactly that case.
| #
| # GNU Make assembles `/bin/sh -c `>pwned` "echo one > out"`. The shell it hands
| # that to runs the backquote — leaving `pwned` behind — substitutes it to
| # nothing, drops the empty field, and runs `/bin/sh -c "echo one > out"`.
| #
| # THE GATE IS THE FILE `out`. Ronin used to ask only whether the SHELL was the
| # default and then exec an argv, so the empty backquote word was a real
| # argument that `-c` took for the command: the recipe became `$0` and never
| # ran, `out` was never written, and the build exited 0 saying nothing. The
| # recording holds a build in which `out` was written.
| SHELL := /bin/sh
| .SHELLFLAGS := -c `>pwned`
| all:
| 	@echo one > out
file out touched 1
| one
file pwned touched 0
